       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order. Amlodipine 20 mg once daily is double the FDA-labeled maximum of 10 mg per day. Nothing in the details you gave (normal kidney and liver function, 70 kg) would justify going above that. Doses over 10 mg add more edema, hypotension, and reflex tachycardia without much more blood pressure lowering.\n\nHere are some options:\n- **Amlodipine 10 mg PO once daily.** This is the maximum standard dose.\n- **Amlodipine 5 mg PO once daily.** This is the usual starting dose, and it can be titrated up after 1\u20132 weeks. It's also the safer choice if the patient is frail or elderly, or if you're starting from scratch.\n- **A second agent.** If 10 mg isn't enough, adding an ACE inhibitor, ARB, or thiazide is generally better than going past the amlodipine maximum.\n\nPlease check whether 20 mg was a typo. It could also be a mix-up with another drug or with a total daily dose. If you confirm the dose you want, or if your attending has deliberately asked for a dose above the maximum, I can enter it. In that case, I'd add a note to the order documenting the rationale. Which would you like?"
      }
     ],
